{{smaller block|The most brittle ''water-carriage'' was used among the Egyptians, who, as Strabo saith, would sail sometimes in boats made of earthenware. {{right|''Arbuthnot.''}}}}

'''water-carrier''' (wâ′tėr-kar″i-ėr), ''n.'' One who or that which carries water; specifically, an arrangement of wires or the like on which a bucket of water, raised from a well, etc., may be conveyed wherever required, as to a house.—{{smaller|'''Water-carriers’ paralysis''', paralysis of the musculospiral nerve.}}

'''water-cart''' (wâ′tėr-kärt), ''n.'' A cart carrying water for sale or for watering streets, gardens, etc. {{smaller|For the latter purpose the cart bears a large cask or tank containing water, which, by means of a tube or tubes perforated with holes, is sprinkled on roads and streets to prevent dust from rising, or in gardens to water plants.}}

'''water-cask''' (wâ′tėr-kȧsk), ''n.'' A strong light cask used for transporting drinking-water, especially on sea-going ships. Compare ''water-tank'' and ''breaker''.

